\description{
\if{html}{\out{<a href="https://adrientaudiere.github.io/MiscMetabar/articles/Rules.html#lifecycle">}} \if{html}{\out{<img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/lifecycle-experimental-orange" alt="lifecycle-experimental">}}\if{html}{\out{</a>}}

Group the taxa of a \code{\link[phyloseq]{phyloseq-class}} object into
\emph{network sequence clusters} (NSC), following the sequence-similarity network
(SSN) approach of Forster et al. (2019). All pairs of reference sequences are
compared with \code{vsearch --allpairs_global}, every pair at least \code{id} similar
becomes an edge of an undirected graph, and each connected component of that
graph is one cluster. Taxa of the same component are then merged with
\code{\link[MiscMetabar:merge_taxa_vec]{MiscMetabar::merge_taxa_vec()}}.

A connected component is a \emph{single-linkage} group: two sequences end up in
the same NSC as soon as a chain of pairwise similarities links them, even
when they are themselves below \code{id}. This is the point of the method — it
recovers the loose, chained variation of a species that a fixed centroid
threshold splits apart — but it also means an NSC can be much wider than
\code{id}, and that a single bridging sequence can fuse two clusters. Use
\code{\link[=ssn_glom_scan_pq]{ssn_glom_scan_pq()}} to see how the number of clusters responds to \code{id}
before committing to a value.

Unlike \code{\link[MiscMetabar:postcluster_pq]{MiscMetabar::postcluster_pq()}}, which assigns each sequence to a
centroid, and unlike \code{\link[=phylo_glom_pq]{phylo_glom_pq()}}, which cuts a tree, \code{ssn_glom_pq()}
needs no centroid and no tree: only the \code{refseq} slot.
}
\details{
vsearch is an external program and is not installed by phylopq. Use
\code{\link[MiscMetabar:is_vsearch_installed]{MiscMetabar::is_vsearch_installed()}} to check for it and
\code{\link[MiscMetabar:install_vsearch]{MiscMetabar::install_vsearch()}} to install it.

\code{--allpairs_global} aligns every pair of sequences, so the run is
quadratic in the number of taxa. It is comfortable for a few thousand
taxa and slow well beyond that; subset or pre-cluster first in that case.

Taxa that vsearch reports no hit for form their own single-taxon cluster
rather than being dropped, so no abundance is lost by the agglomeration.
}
